ally on us to really  think about  the dashers the gig workers that we work  with and and think  more broadly than just our business but  also how we're impacting other people's  lives so that was  a really good reminder to have  and because of the pandemic tony i think  men  all of us had to face unexpected  challenges this year so  in that spirit i have a closing question  for you before we turn to student q a  what are the principles that you rely on  as a leader  during the toughest times  well i think the first is you know to to  do the right thing which often  is probably the hard thing and um  and you know during this pandemic um the  the right  and the hard thing to do was to shelve  some of our business plans  and actually make sure that that  the community was safe that the dashers  would be safe  that merchants would have liquidity that  we can help the community any  organization that may need our help  um whether it's you know delivering  meals just children who no longer can  get them anymore because schools are  closed  um or to give healthcare workers you  know free meals because you know they're  working 24 7.  so that's the first thing the second  thing is  that you have to have a bias for action  um because you can't let the uncertainty  if you will  necessarily  i guess stop all things or stop all  activity  and most of the time that that bias for  action  means it's just focusing on what you can  control it's not trying to say  by doing this we're going to control  some outcome but by doing this that we  will have some influence  and maybe you know that work  combined with maybe you know luck a lot  of times  that that's what will yield the the good  outcome  and you know for us um for example  when we at the beginning the pandemic it  wasn't obvious to me  that even the restaurant kitchens would  be allowed to stay open  actually if you looked at what happened  across the world some countries whether  it's china  and some others like the uk actually  shut down restaurants  altogether completely including the  kitchens which meant the takeout or the  delivery  business would be stopped as well and um  those are things you have to be prepared  for and so you and  this is what i mean when i say you can't  influence that you you can work  alongside  you know governments and elected  officials on that um but but you  you can't control that outcome but you  have to be prepared for you know what  the consequences are  um i think the third thing is um  you have to trust your team um because  no one person is going to get you  through  a pen you know any sort of difficult  situation  and certainly not a pandemic you know to  give you a sense of what it felt like  doordash i mean it pretty much was 24 7  from march through june where  we had you know twice a day meetings  across probably 20 or so work streams  when you have something like that it's  not possible to know  everything and control that decision  making  and so i think a lot of this  obviously you know starts from  preparations that you made a long time  ago such as  building great people up allowing them  to make  many decisions prior to when a big  moment like this happens  um but but you have to trust the team  and you have to trust the process that  they've built  um to solve the hard problems and that's  what i'm most proud of you know when i  look back at um you know 2020 it wasn't  necessarily  you know what happened with an ipo it  was you know how we responded during  um a difficult time for everyone  that's really valuable advice and tony  thank you for sharing your grit  your scrappiness and growth mindset with  us  with that we'll now turn to student q a  i believe that  jenna is up first my question is about  cities  and how they'll evolve and so you've  mentioned a little bit about the future  of work about sort of changing  regulatory environments i'm curious how  else do you see cities evolving over the  next 10 years and  what is doordash's role or place within  that evolution  sure well you know the first thing i  would say is i think cities are always  going to get better  i mean i i think um i forget uh who  studied this maybe  um i'm gonna blank on the author but  um but but cities have been one of the  most enduring  i guess organizations that have ever  made it through the history of time you  know far more enduring than businesses  have and and i think again  you know i mentioned earlier that most  of gdp  still happens inside local communities  you know i think in the us that's  north of 50 that number has been true in  pretty much every decade that it's been  measured  and um i think it shows you the  resilience of how cities go through  you know obviously economic cycles as  well as even you know pandemic cycles  i'm very optimistic  and i think you know what you're going  to see is i think you're going to see  more consistency  across cities i think we used to maybe  think oh well  you must live in a certain city in order  to access certain things you must live  in new york city to access  delivery you must live in san francisco  to work in  technology i think you're going to see  more of that spread  you're going to see a greater  distribution of all the things that i  think we each want you know convenience  for example or access to different types  of jobs  in more places and and this kind of  growth of almost hubs if you will of  all the things that because we're all  human we all want similar things at the  end of the day  um i think it's going to be more  consistent  not less consistent um and so i i think  our role  is to play our small part in making sure  that we can  make every small medium and large  business you know brick and mortar  business  make this transformation from doing  pretty much everything from customer  acquisition to customer service  inside stores to a world where they do  this  also with digital channels and so  um i think we have a long ways to go um  and  but we're excited about the journey  ahead and what other industries or  verticals could you see  doordash's last mile fulfillment model  be a pl  be applied well and why  sure well um again crawl walk run as i  like to say  um and it's it's it's tough doing one  thing well  and and so as you kind of go forward you  want to sequence that appropriately but  uh well one of the reasons why we  started with um  there are a few reasons why we started  with restaurants um you know one of the  reasons why we started with restaurants  was um you know if the goal ultimately  is to bring you everything inside your  city  um in minutes not hours or days we  thought it would um  make sense to start with what we had  guessed to be the highest frequency  category first  so that we can have lots of activity  because when you have lots of activity  in the same geography you have um  basically the the greatest density of  work if you will the greatest density of  work is what allows you to be more  efficient  and also achieve a lower cost  way of bringing things to places the  other reason why we started with food  is um we knew that something like  restaurant food is very perishable  obviously  you have minutes to get this right and  if you get it wrong you kind of have to  solve it then and there  you can't you know wait to you know give  you your password the next day or  something it's not like that  you kind of have to solve it there and  then and so  um we thought that if we can tackle the  hardest problem first  that other categories at least from a  pure um  last mile logistics perspective um would  would be easier to do obviously if you  have more time to deliver something less  perishable that's easier  than more time pressure and greater  perishability and so  um you know for us you know we found  that to be true as as  now we're starting to deliver  convenience items from places like  cbs walgreens 7-eleven as we're  delivering grocery items from places  like smart and final or  meyer hyvee and other places um that  that that seems to be true  but we have a lot of work to do i mean i  think one of the hardest things of what  we have to do is we have to digitize the  physical world  um there does not exist for instance  some easy structured catalog  where you know how long it takes to bake  a deep dish pizza  versus making a salad or how many apples  are left in aisle six  or what are the prices for this item as  they are changing  maybe by the hour um by certain stores  because of a promotion or something like  that  and so um or i mean i know we're in a  pandemic but post pandemic where's the  last  you know parking space in san francisco  or los angeles i mean these are tricky  questions  um where we first have to almost study  the world first  um before we start um hopefully you know  bring value to different people inside  that world so  that's still a big part of the journey  for us right now  but i'm confident that you know based on  where we started  that that gives us quite a lot of  opportunities to deliver everything else  thank you both for the questions tony  i'd like to wrap up with a custom review  from the top lightning round  i think we have maybe 30 seconds left so  i'll be quick  um it's only three questions the first  question is  what is your favorite class of the gsb  i i don't know if it's taught anymore um  it was called  i think it was called aligning startups  in their markets taught by andy  ratcliffe  um and mark leslie i don't know if that  class is still there but  that was um that was a highlight and  what is your go-to food delivery order  oh actually i don't have one so  my my my wife and i we actually try a  different place  um uh almost every time when we were so  we've ordered from about 1100 places so  far  in the bay area i guess the most recent  order  was a um a new sushi place  that we discovered in san francisco  called sasa japanese restaurant  nice last question in just one sentence  what would be your  word of advice for aspiring  entrepreneurs at the gsb  well if you're an aspiring entrepreneur  i i i i would say  find something you're obsessive about  and um  that's and and then um that's where i  would start  because um you know the journey to  building anything  of um meaning i believe will take  years maybe decades and so i think  unless you are  maniacally focused on it i i think it is  difficult  um to maybe make it all the way through  all the um  ups and downs and so find something find  something that you're obsessive about  with that tony it's been truly a  pleasure thank you again for joining us  of course thanks for having me  you

